  "query_preview": "..\n\nThrough next Saturday...\n\nLight snow has tapered to light snow showers and flurries, and \nhave expired the Winter Weather Advisory for the Bighorn Canyon, \nSouthern Big Horn and Sheridan Foothills. Any wet roads will turn \nicy after sunset and we may see fog tonight...a heads up for \ntravelers.\n\nSatellite imagery shows an upper low over southern Saskatchewan\nand cyclonic flow over a broad region including southern AB, MT\nand northern WY. There is a Pacific low off the coast and moisture\nspreading inland. This evening, light snow showers will linger\nover the mountains courtesy of the cyclonic flow and some diurnal\ninstability. Later tonight into Sunday, isentropic ascent spreads\nover our cwa from the west and model soundings show mid level\nmoisture intersecting the DGZ, on top of low level light/easterly\nwinds, and this looks sufficient for generating flurries over\nwestern foothills/lower elevations. Most models do not show any\nQPF outside of the Beartooth-Absarokas, which should see snow\nshowers and an inch or less of accumulation. Have adjusted pop/wx\ngrids accordingly. Otherwise, most of the region will be dry/cool\nSunday & Sunday night. Another weak shortwave tracks through the \nregion Monday bringing snow showers mainly to southern mountains, \nbut strongest forcing is expected to drive well to our south so do\nnot expect any real impacts. Generally speaking, look for below \nnormal temps, light winds and patchy nighttime fog tonight through\nMonday. Sunday looks like the coldest day with many locations \nlikely to stay below 30F.\n\nThe regime changes to one of higher heights and downslope\ngradients starting late Monday night & Tuesday. This will allow\nSW-W winds to increase along the western foothills. In fact,\nconditions may favor gusts nearing 50 mph at the gap locations by\ndaybreak Tuesday. This is supported by the gradient and stability,\nbut mid level flow is expected to be modest (~30kts at 700mb).\nAfter some diurnal decrease during the day Tuesday, expect the \ngradient to tighten again early Wednesday. Though foothills winds \nincrease by midweek, the stronger potential across all western \nfoothills looks like the end of the week and next weekend as \nwesterly flow aloft strengthens. Something to monitor over the \nnext several days.\n\nNext week is looking mostly dry with the possible exception of a\nPacific wave Thursday. Models remain in poor agreement with the\nstrength of this feature, but a stronger solution would bring some\nlower elevation rain showers, a period of mountain snow on west\naspects, and increased wind. Regarding temperatures, look for\nlow-mid 40s by Tuesday climbing to the 50s by the end of the week,\nand cannot rule out 60F by Saturday, which ensembles show as the \nwarmest of the next 7 days. Safe to say the lower elevation snow \ncover we just received will melt. Also, Thanksgiving is looking \nwarmer than normal and potentially breezy.\n\nBeyond next Saturday the ensembles are in fair agreement showing \n(in a general sense) falling heights and a cooling trend.\n\nJKL",
